00:23:42.230 --> 00:23:45.259
- Okay let's talk about friendship.

00:23:45.259 --> 00:23:46.258
With robots.

00:23:46.258 --> 00:23:50.400
- So Feldman brought to our
attention a Medium article

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:54.220
by Evan Selinger called Can
We Be Friends With Robots

00:23:54.220 --> 00:23:58.840
and I think he pitched
it because friendship,

00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:01.620
friendship's a thing for us, you know?

00:24:01.620 --> 00:24:02.453
We're friends.

00:24:02.453 --> 00:24:03.340
- Right.

00:24:03.340 --> 00:24:06.160
- It's been happenin' for a long time.

00:24:06.160 --> 00:24:07.923
Are we experts in friendship?

00:24:08.780 --> 00:24:11.550
I don't know, on one hand
I feel like we're experts

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:12.900
at our friendship, on the other hand,

00:24:12.900 --> 00:24:15.790
I feel like there's
still so much to explore

00:24:15.790 --> 00:24:17.560
that I actually don't know

00:24:17.560 --> 00:24:19.520
if we're experts at our own friendship.

00:24:19.520 --> 00:24:22.340
- I would definitely say I'm
not an expert at friendship.

00:24:22.340 --> 00:24:24.270
- At our friendship, like just--

00:24:24.270 --> 00:24:25.670
- Yeah.
- Understanding it.

00:24:28.220 --> 00:24:33.220
So let's, I'm interested
in filtering our feelings

00:24:34.056 --> 00:24:39.056
about our capacity to have
true friendship with a robot.

00:24:41.980 --> 00:24:45.270
Maybe through our experience
in our own friendship.

00:24:45.270 --> 00:24:48.230
I think there are some
parallels, so spoiler alert.

00:24:48.230 --> 00:24:49.580
A little background though.

00:24:51.090 --> 00:24:54.210
In his article, and in a
few others that I read,

00:24:54.210 --> 00:24:59.210
they mention the way that
Aristotle talked about friendship

00:25:00.040 --> 00:25:02.840
and he actually, you
know who Aristotle is?

00:25:02.840 --> 00:25:04.763
- I've heard of him.
- He's a philosopher.

00:25:07.411 --> 00:25:09.700
He talked about three
different types of friendships.

00:25:09.700 --> 00:25:12.983
Utility friendships which are basically,

00:25:14.430 --> 00:25:16.660
some examples are like
business partnerships,

00:25:16.660 --> 00:25:20.340
alliances, like a Survivor alliance.

00:25:20.340 --> 00:25:23.039
Speak the parlance of reality television.

00:25:23.039 --> 00:25:26.180
A friendship where you, you
know, you get something out

00:25:26.180 --> 00:25:28.720
of it that otherwise you wouldn't get.

00:25:28.720 --> 00:25:31.750
- I call those transactional
friendships and I think--

00:25:31.750 --> 00:25:33.140
- Yeah you can improve on Aristotle.

00:25:33.140 --> 00:25:37.180
- I think that 90% or
more of relationships

00:25:37.180 --> 00:25:39.430
in Los Angeles are transactional.

00:25:39.430 --> 00:25:40.370
- Yeah.

00:25:40.370 --> 00:25:44.978
What can you give me life access to?

00:25:44.978 --> 00:25:46.423
- Yeah.
- How can I,

00:25:47.449 --> 00:25:52.170
a second Aristotle friendship
is friendships of pleasure.

00:25:52.170 --> 00:25:53.183
- Hmm.
- Ooh.

00:25:54.500 --> 00:25:56.860
Activity buddies, you know.

00:25:56.860 --> 00:25:59.030
Hey, let's do a sport.

00:25:59.030 --> 00:26:00.470
Let's do a hobby.

00:26:00.470 --> 00:26:03.270
Let's build a Popsicle stick castle.

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:04.470
- We did that once.

00:26:04.470 --> 00:26:05.480
With the RAs.

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:06.730
- Yeah, yeah.

00:26:06.730 --> 00:26:08.450
- The Royal Ambassadors.
- Yeah they had this

00:26:08.450 --> 00:26:09.820
whole room and you'd just go in there

00:26:09.820 --> 00:26:12.266
and you'd just keep adding
to the Popsicle stick fort.

00:26:12.266 --> 00:26:13.373
Pretty cool.
- And then we would go

00:26:13.373 --> 00:26:15.120
in there after that and
at least I would go in

00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:16.920
and rip it apart a little bit.

00:26:16.920 --> 00:26:18.057
- Really?
- Yeah.

00:26:18.057 --> 00:26:19.390
- Did you notice how every once in awhile

00:26:19.390 --> 00:26:20.410
you'd go in there and it would be like

00:26:20.410 --> 00:26:21.470
oh another part had fallen.

00:26:21.470 --> 00:26:22.490
- Yeah it was like they're lettin'

00:26:22.490 --> 00:26:23.547
the pre-schoolers in here.

00:26:23.547 --> 00:26:24.620
Why are they lettin'
the younger kids in here

00:26:24.620 --> 00:26:27.190
when nobody, keep an
eye on the younger kids.

00:26:27.190 --> 00:26:28.196
- It was me.
- They keep coming in.

00:26:28.196 --> 00:26:30.480
(chuckles)

00:26:30.480 --> 00:26:31.313
So yeah.
- I was slowly

00:26:31.313 --> 00:26:32.490
destroying our creation.

00:26:32.490 --> 00:26:34.640
- That's when it's, hey,
we just have fun together.

00:26:34.640 --> 00:26:36.000
It's all about doing.

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:41.000
I think in general, guys
in my limited observation,

00:26:44.420 --> 00:26:48.700
are more willingly settle
in or settle for this type

00:26:48.700 --> 00:26:51.653
of friendship, that's just like hey,

00:26:53.060 --> 00:26:55.370
we could shoot the breeze,
we could grab a brewski

00:26:55.370 --> 00:26:59.310
or we can hike a mountain
or we can play tag football,

00:26:59.310 --> 00:27:01.280
or again, Popsicle sticks.

00:27:01.280 --> 00:27:03.380
And that's good enough.

00:27:03.380 --> 00:27:08.380
But Aristotle talks about
a third type of friendship

00:27:08.630 --> 00:27:11.593
called friendship of the good which is,

00:27:13.146 --> 00:27:15.670
perhaps a more complete friendship I think

00:27:15.670 --> 00:27:18.420
it could be described as,
it's based on mutual goodwill

00:27:18.420 --> 00:27:21.508
and unselfish desire to help
the other person become his

00:27:21.508 --> 00:27:24.463
or her best possible self.

00:27:25.330 --> 00:27:28.770
So there's a selfless component

00:27:28.770 --> 00:27:32.640
and like a legitimate care for somebody.

00:27:32.640 --> 00:27:34.513
There's some buddy love.
- Mm.

00:27:35.710 --> 00:27:37.090
- Now...

00:27:38.896 --> 00:27:41.441
I think we can filter
the robot conversation

00:27:41.441 --> 00:27:44.480
as we delve into it through that.

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:49.113
- Well first of all, I
think that it's easy,

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:53.990
it's easy to see that robots
fall, without a doubt fall

00:27:53.990 --> 00:27:55.163
into category one.

00:27:56.810 --> 00:27:57.963
Friendships of utility.

00:27:58.960 --> 00:28:02.623
Because when we think about
robots, we think about,

00:28:04.270 --> 00:28:06.143
what was the one from The Jetsons?

00:28:07.405 --> 00:28:09.557
- The maid, Rosie.
- Rosie?

00:28:10.630 --> 00:28:12.420
You know, Rosie's the maid.

00:28:12.420 --> 00:28:14.760
She's doing something and of course,

00:28:14.760 --> 00:28:16.530
you may have a relationship
but the relationship is

00:28:16.530 --> 00:28:18.470
in the context of what
Rosie can do for you

00:28:18.470 --> 00:28:21.800
so without a doubt that those
types of friendships exist.

00:28:21.800 --> 00:28:23.570
- But interesting that--
- Can exist.

00:28:23.570 --> 00:28:26.000
- I think they demonstrated very well

00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:27.850
because I just bought
it hook line and sinker

00:28:27.850 --> 00:28:30.150
that she was a member of the family.

00:28:30.150 --> 00:28:35.069
But she's a member of the
family created specifically

00:28:35.069 --> 00:28:39.840
to just clean up after
'em and like, you know,

00:28:39.840 --> 00:28:44.220
I never saw Astro's poop anywhere
now that I think about it.

00:28:44.220 --> 00:28:46.600
- Can't show poop on cartoon television.

00:28:46.600 --> 00:28:48.390
- That's bull crap, man.

00:28:48.390 --> 00:28:50.660
Well it's Astro crap.

00:28:50.660 --> 00:28:52.520
- Can't show it, it's a rule.

00:28:52.520 --> 00:28:54.530
- I actually think they could show it

00:28:54.530 --> 00:28:57.610
but Rosie was so good at her job

00:28:57.610 --> 00:29:01.140
that it was never there for
more than a split second,

00:29:01.140 --> 00:29:03.040
like she had an innate ability.

00:29:03.040 --> 00:29:05.133
Her artificial intelligence was honed.

00:29:06.390 --> 00:29:08.940
But I considered her a
member of the family.

00:29:08.940 --> 00:29:10.970
I mean Elroy certainly loved her.

00:29:10.970 --> 00:29:12.570
- Well of course, I mean--

00:29:12.570 --> 00:29:14.610
- So I do think it is a form of friendship

00:29:14.610 --> 00:29:17.000
because as you talked about
the transactional nature,

00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:19.462
it tends to cheapen it so
much that I actually question

00:29:19.462 --> 00:29:21.453
can you even call that friendship?

00:29:23.321 --> 00:29:25.520
Are you friends with Alexa?

00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:27.780
- Not Alexa.
- No.

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:30.090
Or the Google Siri person.

00:29:30.090 --> 00:29:32.330
- The Google Siri
person, that's confusing.

00:29:32.330 --> 00:29:33.420
- It's two different people, right?

00:29:33.420 --> 00:29:34.693
- Right.
- Google doesn't have a name.

00:29:34.693 --> 00:29:36.340
They just call it Google.

00:29:36.340 --> 00:29:38.803
- Right.
- Which I prefer.

00:29:39.740 --> 00:29:42.420
Because it sets a strong boundary.

00:29:42.420 --> 00:29:43.810
It's not like I'm pretending that this,

00:29:43.810 --> 00:29:45.240
I don't have to call it a human name.

00:29:45.240 --> 00:29:46.550
- And that's an interesting
thing 'cause one

00:29:46.550 --> 00:29:49.400
of the articles I was reading
was the difference between,

00:29:50.410 --> 00:29:53.280
if you contrast sort of western approaches

00:29:53.280 --> 00:29:58.280
to sort of embodied AI
to the way they see it

00:30:00.160 --> 00:30:01.970
in Japan, for instance.

00:30:01.970 --> 00:30:04.270
You just sort of, I
don't know exactly where

00:30:04.270 --> 00:30:06.000
that's coming from and
you're like I feel like

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:07.800
there needs to be a boundary.

00:30:07.800 --> 00:30:09.900
- Mm-hmm.
- But that boundary

00:30:09.900 --> 00:30:14.273
does not exist in Japan, the
way they've embraced robots

00:30:16.097 --> 00:30:18.183
and the personality of robots, in fact,

00:30:21.180 --> 00:30:23.240
Sony's, I think it's AIBO.

00:30:23.240 --> 00:30:25.423
Is that the dog robot?

00:30:28.520 --> 00:30:31.320
When one of those robots in Japan,

00:30:31.320 --> 00:30:34.439
those robots are so revered in Japan

00:30:34.439 --> 00:30:38.290
that when you can no longer repair them,

00:30:38.290 --> 00:30:41.670
they have like a funeral for the dog.

00:30:41.670 --> 00:30:43.003
- Really?
- Yeah.

00:30:44.180 --> 00:30:45.511
Yeah, AIBO.

00:30:45.511 --> 00:30:46.910
- A-I-B-O.

00:30:46.910 --> 00:30:50.870
- And that's something
that we're like what?

00:30:50.870 --> 00:30:52.317
Like typically I think the way that we,

00:30:52.317 --> 00:30:54.720
and I don't know which
one, we had that robot dog

00:30:54.720 --> 00:30:56.290
on the show at one point.

00:30:56.290 --> 00:30:57.560
I don't know if that's the same one.

00:30:57.560 --> 00:31:00.270
I can't imagine that dog,
'cause the way that would go

00:31:00.270 --> 00:31:02.817
at my house, because I
think we actually had that.

00:31:02.817 --> 00:31:05.480
- You can't converse with
that dog we had on the show.

00:31:05.480 --> 00:31:08.010
- Well you can't converse with a dog.

00:31:08.010 --> 00:31:10.260
They speak dog anyway.

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:11.715
So I think this dog just does dog things.

00:31:11.715 --> 00:31:14.010
- Yes you can, you can say sit to a dog.

00:31:14.010 --> 00:31:16.200
- Right, well I think you can say,

00:31:16.200 --> 00:31:19.740
regardless of whether this is
the one that I've seen before,

00:31:19.740 --> 00:31:21.475
the way it would go at
the McLaughlin household

00:31:21.475 --> 00:31:24.450
is we would have thing and
everybody would think it was cool

00:31:24.450 --> 00:31:29.450
for like 48 hours and then
there would be like two months

00:31:30.410 --> 00:31:33.770
of every other day somebody
would do something with it

00:31:33.770 --> 00:31:36.340
and then it would be put in a closet

00:31:36.340 --> 00:31:37.410
and then it would be forgotten about

00:31:37.410 --> 00:31:41.470
and then it would be
thrown away or Craigslisted

00:31:41.470 --> 00:31:42.640
or yard saled.

00:31:42.640 --> 00:31:44.100
- But if Siri--
- Yard sold.

00:31:44.100 --> 00:31:48.903
- Or Alexa could be
moved into a mobile body,

00:31:51.670 --> 00:31:54.150
let's not say it looks like a human

00:31:54.150 --> 00:31:56.193
but let's just say it's more android.

00:31:57.104 --> 00:31:59.713
It's a little more personified.

00:32:00.780 --> 00:32:03.700
So we're not venturing
into the uncanny valley

00:32:03.700 --> 00:32:05.360
with some weirdness but it's just like,

00:32:05.360 --> 00:32:07.283
like a Rosie situation.
- Yeah.

00:32:09.490 --> 00:32:13.160
- I think psychologically,
you would start to form bonds.

00:32:13.160 --> 00:32:14.830
I mean, even--
- Of course you would.

00:32:14.830 --> 00:32:18.540
- Even when Lando goes to sleep he's like,

00:32:18.540 --> 00:32:21.240
okay Google, tell me a bedtime story.

00:32:21.240 --> 00:32:26.240
And she, it, whatever, will
tell a bedtime story sometimes.

00:32:28.060 --> 00:32:31.880
Well, every single time he
asks without fail, actually.

00:32:31.880 --> 00:32:32.830
- A different one?

00:32:32.830 --> 00:32:33.690
- Yeah a different one.

00:32:33.690 --> 00:32:35.520
- For how long do the stories go?

00:32:35.520 --> 00:32:37.030
- Maybe eight minutes.

00:32:37.030 --> 00:32:37.863
- Really?
- Yeah.

00:32:37.863 --> 00:32:39.170
- Interesting.
- I mean he doesn't do,

00:32:39.170 --> 00:32:44.170
he doesn't do it every night
but if okay Google walked up,

00:32:44.460 --> 00:32:49.460
like strutted over, did a
little hammer dance over

00:32:49.840 --> 00:32:53.540
to the bed side and then told the story,

00:32:53.540 --> 00:32:58.540
all of a sudden, he's forming
a bond with this person.

00:32:59.190 --> 00:33:01.050
And that moves to the second one--

00:33:01.050 --> 00:33:02.883
- Are you, you said the bond.

00:33:03.868 --> 00:33:06.580
You seem to be, I'm just
saying, I'm picking up

00:33:06.580 --> 00:33:09.850
on some suspicion on
whether or not this bond

00:33:09.850 --> 00:33:11.210
would be a good thing or not.

00:33:11.210 --> 00:33:14.750
- I felt weird, it feels
weirder to me to say Alexa

00:33:14.750 --> 00:33:16.900
than it does to say okay Google.

00:33:16.900 --> 00:33:17.733
- But why?

00:33:20.790 --> 00:33:24.893
- Because it's, I know I'm
not talking to a person.

00:33:25.780 --> 00:33:27.990
I'm talking to a speaker.

00:33:27.990 --> 00:33:29.820
So it seems kinda stupid.

00:33:29.820 --> 00:33:31.470
It's like if I have friends over,

00:33:32.351 --> 00:33:33.970
I feel like a douche if
